  "explanation": [
    "80 MHz doubles the serial pixel clock relative to40 MHz, not the CPU, RAM bandwidth or the entire frame pipeline.",
    "Core0 produces damage-tracked16-row raster stripes with blur/history; Core1 rotates output into8-row DMA chunks and submits display commands. Rendering and transfer overlap.",
    "Each chunk has CASET/RASET command work; the local ESP-IDF parameter-write path waits for previous color transactions. API elapsed time therefore includes prior DMA waiting.",
    "In the captured slow frames, UI stripe wait is tiny, the flush worker has substantial queue idle time, and final drain is short. This points to frame production/raster/history as the larger limit here, rather than sustained pixel-wire throughput.",
    "The authored40 Hz frame tables remain separate from60 Hz presentation. Some table-driven effects still sample40 authored poses per second; springs and elapsed motion can be sampled more often."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Single typical upward swipe per app per mode; this is an exploratory profile, not a workload-wide accuracy or soak claim.",
    "Quiet and instrumented windows are separate samples with potentially different list positions/cache state. Their difference is not a controlled estimate of instrumentation overhead.",
    "Detailed frames are the adjacent pair around the slowest painted completion interval in each instrumented window, not an average frame.",
    "Render/raster/API/wait counters are elapsed wall durations, not hardware CPU-cycle accounting. History and damage comparison are nested in raster. Worker times overlap UI work.",
    "The transfer envelope includes gaps and command/scheduling work. Exact physical wire-active time, optical refresh/latch timing and photon timing were not measured.",
    "40 MHz values in the clock model are ideal payload calculations, not a current measured40 MHz run. No measured2× speedup claim is made.",
    "Payload lower bound assumes four data lanes at the requested clock; commands, gaps and all rendering are excluded. Dirty/round-clipped bytes differ from a full rectangular frame.",
    "Inter-frame gap includes housekeeping and pacing; it is not a pure sleep timer. All probe and awake flags were restored, preferences matched and no new retained faults appeared."
  ],
